# SINGLE-OWNER SUB-PLACE NEG — PLACE-SHAPE axis: tuple element, UNDERSCORE-ALIAS
# spelling `t._1`. THE ROW THAT WAS NOT FIXED BY THE GATE WIDENING ALONE.
#
# `t.1` and `t._1` are the same place. The parser builds `TupleFieldAccess` only
# for the literal-integer form; `._1` goes through `expect_name()` and arrives as
# `Expr::FieldAccess { field: "_1" }`. The safety walk's `lvalue_value_type` then
# needed a struct `DefId` to index `struct_field_names`, a tuple has none, so the
# alias typed as unknown and EVERY ownership gate keyed on that resolver walked
# straight past it while catching `t.1` one character away. Fixed by giving the
# alias ONE resolver (`ast::tuple_field_alias_index`, Layering rule 3),
# read by the typechecker AND the safety walk — the shape the self-host lane has
# always had (`tuple_field_index`, self_host_typechecker/infer.gg).
#
# Break-and-watch: comment out `lvalue_value_type`'s `ResolvedType::Tuple` arm
# and this cell goes rc 0 (and double-frees) while its `t.1` sibling stays rc 1.
#
# Omitted cell: bare INDEX places are deferred behind the callee-borrow ruling
# (todo/t1225).
#
# CELL: type=Callable · place=TUPLE element (`._1`) · position=bind · capture=int.
# RED at 7bf11017e: `gg check` ACCEPTED (rc 0), two reads double-free.
# ggdef: ABSTAIN (todo/t1227).

Callable[int()] mk(int n):
    return (): n + 1

void main():
    (int, Callable[int()]) t = (1, mk(40))
    Callable[int()] g = t._1
    print(g())
